  "query_preview": "..\n(Wednesday night through Monday)\nIssued at 224 AM EDT Tue Sep 15 2026\n\nThe extended forecast for North and Central Georgia remains largely \nuneventful. Subsidence associated with mid-level ridging and a \nsurface high extending up the East Coast should largely suppress \nany meaningful precipitation for most through Thursday. Global \nmodels and ensembles suggest ridging at the mid-levels begins to \ngradually break down/retrograde leading to eventual return of \nmoisture by the weekend into early next week. Precipitation \nchances remain low (30 percent or less) through Saturday, but \nwe're likely to see chances increase slightly early next week \n(30-40 percent) as southerly flow strengthens. As far as \ntemperatures go, the heat will stick around with us for a little \nwhile longer. Temperatures are forecast to rise into the 90s each \nafternoon (70s/80s in the mountains) and fall to the 60s/low 70s \neach night/early morning.",
